Acute Viral Pharyngitis
Common Infections and Afflictions
Erythematous pharynx, Cough and coryza, Absence of exudate

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-004
Reason for Visit: Severe sore throat and inability to tolerate oral fluids.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, worsening odynophagia over the past 12 hours resulting in a complete cessation of oral fluid intake, alongside generalized weakness. Urgent home assessment is clinically indicated to assess hydration status, provide immediate systemic analgesia, and rule out suppurative complications such as peritonsillar abscess or deep neck space infections. The imminent risk of clinical dehydration and the potential for rapid airway compromise from a forming abscess demand immediate intervention that cannot be delayed until the next standard operating period (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: It feels like I'm swallowing ground glass, and I haven't been able to drink a single drop of water all day.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced 48 hours ago with mild myalgia, conjunctival irritation, and rhinorrhea. Progressed over the last 24 hours to include a persistent cough and a severe sore throat. The odynophagia has become severe enough to preclude the ingestion of liquids. Patient reports a subjective low-grade fever and generalized fatigue.
Functional Status: Independent, though currently restricted by significant fatigue and localized pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High risk for rapid dehydration if oral intake is not re-established immediately.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Appears fatigued and mildly dehydrated (dry oral mucosa). Evident discomfort when attempting to swallow saliva.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal examination reveals a deeply erythematous pharyngeal mucosa. Tonsils are mildly enlarged bilaterally but definitively lack significant purulent exudate or palatal petechiae. Uvula is midline with no soft palate bulging or asymmetry. Mild, tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy is palpable. Chest is clear to auscultation. Bilateral conjunctival injection noted.
Environmental Observations: Residence is secure. Readily available access to fluids in the kitchen.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Has not taken analgesia due to fear of swallowing pills.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Acute Viral Pharyngitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Streptococcal Pharyngitis - Unlikely given the concurrent presence of prominent viral symptoms (cough, rhinorrhea, conjunctivitis) and the definitive absence of tonsillar exudate and palatal petechiae.
Peritonsillar Abscess - Ruled out definitively due to the midline uvula, absence of trismus (hot potato voice), and lack of unilateral soft palate swelling.
Infectious Mononucleosis - Less likely given the acute timeline and lack of posterior cervical lymphadenopathy or massive tonsillar hypertrophy, though remains a secondary consideration if symptoms prolong.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Dehydration risk living alone)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient's severe odynophagia resulting in a cessation of fluid intake presented an imminent risk of dehydration. An urgent home visit was necessary to clinically exclude severe suppurative complications like a peritonsillar abscess and to institute an immediate, aggressive analgesic regimen. Clinical assessment confirmed acute viral pharyngitis with mild dehydration. By directly administering analgesia and observing the subsequent resumption of oral hydration, an emergency department presentation was averted. This critical intervention and assessment clearly could not wait for standard consulting hours, strictly fulfilling MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Ibuprofen 400mg liquid suspension orally stat. Prescribed Benzydamine hydrochloride 0.15% throat spray or gargle for localized topical anesthesia. Recommended alternating Paracetamol 1000mg and Ibuprofen 400mg strictly every 4-6 hours for continuous pain control.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain hydration despite the pain, take oral analgesia 30 minutes prior to attempting large fluid intake; linking the pain relief directly to the functional task of drinking ensures adequate systemic hydration and prevents hospital admission for IV fluids.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner in 3 days if unable to maintain oral hydration or if fever spikes.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if unable to open the mouth fully (trismus), experiencing severe unilateral throat swelling, drooling, or severe lethargy/dizziness upon standing.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive. Viral pharyngitis does not respond to antibiotic therapy.
Clinical Course: Throat pain typically peaks between days 3 and 5 of the illness and slowly resolves over the following week. Fatigue and cough may linger for up to 14 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate emergency medical attention if you notice your voice changing to sound muffled, if you cannot open your mouth, or if you stop urinating for more than 8 hours.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The severe pain is caused by viral inflammation of the throat lining. Antibiotics are contraindicated. Maximizing over-the-counter pain relief is the only effective strategy to ensure you can drink enough water to stay out of the hospital.
Actionable Steps: Drink cold liquids or suck on ice chips, as the cold provides a temporary numbing effect to the inflamed mucosa. Avoid highly acidic beverages like orange juice which will irritate the tissue.

